# One paragraph per section: what the section establishes and how, 40-90 words (110 with subsections, appendices
# 40-70). Every sentence sits in a span naming the 1-3 paragraphs (or figures) that establish it. Status ai-draft.
# No brief for the one- and two-paragraph subsections 2.3.1-2.3.3 and A.1-A.2 (their parents' briefs and the
# paragraph gists cover them) or for the acknowledgements.
BRIEF = {
    "intro": r"""<p><span data-covers="intro-p1 intro-p2">Beyond the $1/N$ expansion the CFT defines its gravity dual only implicitly, and it is unclear whether that bulk is still a sum over configurations.</span> <span data-covers="intro-p3 intro-p5">For vector models, rewriting the singlet sector through bi-local fields gives a path integral over fields of all spins on one fixed AdS, with no sign of a sum over topologies.</span> <span data-covers="intro-p6 intro-p7">Matching conformal eigenmodes to bulk-to-boundary modes gives the map, and one choice of normalization makes the quadratic action local, with a physical field and a ghost for each spin.</span> <span data-covers="intro-p8 intro-p9">Loops are argued to cancel with explicit counter-terms, and the mass and critical deformations are mapped too.</span></p>""",

    "section-1.1": r"""<p><span data-covers="section-1.1-p2">The main open question is whether a path integral over fields on one fixed AdS survives for other theories and backgrounds. On circles, vector models have phase transitions that are expected to change the bulk topology.</span> <span data-covers="section-1.1-p3">More specific questions: is the classical theory Vasiliev's in some gauge, is it local beyond the AdS radius, and how do the finite-$N$ constraints read in the bulk? Extensions to fermions, $S^d$, $SU(N)$, Chern–Simons couplings, Lorentzian signature and de Sitter space are listed too.</span></p>""",

    "sec-local_to_bilocal": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-local_to_bilocal-p2 sec-local_to_bilocal-p5">Every singlet quantity is a function of the bi-local field $G(x_1,x_2)$, but its components are related: on a lattice of $V$ points, $G$ is a non-negative matrix of rank at most $N$.</span> <span data-covers="section-2.1-p1 section-2.1-p3">When $N\ge V$ only non-negativity survives, and the change of variables adds the single non-local term $(N-V)\,\mathrm{Tr}\log G$.</span> <span data-covers="section-2.1-p4 sec-pert_bilocal-p3">Expanding around the free propagator then gives Feynman rules in $1/N$, with counter-terms proportional to $V$.</span> <span data-covers="sec-bilocal_1_loop-p1 section-2.3.2-p2">These cancel the loops of the free theory, as the paper checks at one loop.</span></p>""",

    "section-2.1": r"""<p><span data-covers="section-2.1-p1">Imposing $G$ through a delta function, written with an auxiliary matrix field, and integrating out the scalars gives the Jacobian of the change of variables, $|\det G|^{N-V}$.</span> <span data-covers="section-2.1-p3 section-2.1-p4">The action therefore gains only $(N-V)\,\mathrm{Tr}\log G$. This term is highly non-local, but apart from its cutoff-dependent piece it is proportional to $N$, so a $1/N$ expansion exists, taking $N$ to infinity before $V$.</span> <span data-covers="section-2.1-p5">For $N<V$ the same formula is meaningless, and the rank constraints must be imposed some other way.</span></p>""",

    "sec-pert_bilocal": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-pert_bilocal-p1">At large $N$ the classical solution of the bi-local action is the free propagator $G_0\propto|x_1-x_2|^{-(d-2)}$, with the term proportional to $V$ treated as a one-loop counter-term.</span> <span data-covers="sec-pert_bilocal-p2">Fluctuations are normalized as $G=G_0+\eta/\sqrt N$, and non-negativity imposes nothing in perturbation theory.</span> <span data-covers="sec-pert_bilocal-p3">Expanding the logarithm gives the Feynman rules: a propagator that is a product of two free propagators, $n$-point vertices of order $N^{1-n/2}$, and counter-term vertices carrying a factor $V$.</span></p>""",

    "sec-bilocal_1_loop": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-bilocal_1_loop-p1">Connected correlators of the free theory come from a single loop of Wick contractions and are exactly proportional to $N$, so in the $1/N$ expansion every loop correction must vanish.</span> <span data-covers="section-2.3.1-p1 section-2.3.2-p2">At one loop the tadpole cancels the linear counter-term, and in the two-point function the cubic, quartic and counter-term diagrams cancel power by power of $V$.</span> <span data-covers="section-2.3.3-p1 section-2.3.3-p2">At any order a diagram is free contractions times powers of $V$, but the paper finds no explicit all-orders proof; the cancellation follows only implicitly from the exact change of variables.</span></p>""",

    "sec-bi_local_to_conformal": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-bi_local_to_conformal-p1 sec-conformal_basis-p1">The fluctuation $\eta$ is expanded in conformal three-point functions with an operator of dimension $\Delta$ and spin $J$, the Clebsch–Gordan coefficients of the conformal group. The analysis is done for a generalized free field of any dimension $\Delta_0$.</span> <span data-covers="sec-conformal_basis-p5 sec-conformal_basis-p7">The expansion converges directly only for $\Delta_0>d/4$; below that, as for free fields in $d<4$, the contour in $\Delta$ must be deformed to keep the physical poles.</span> <span data-covers="quadFree-p1 quadFree-p2">In the new variables $C_{\Delta,J}$ the free quadratic action is diagonal, and inverting it reproduces the free four-point function.</span></p>""",

    "sec-conformal_basis": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-conformal_basis-p1 sec-conformal_basis-p3">Three-point functions of the scalar pair with an operator of spin $J$ and dimension $\Delta=\frac d2+is$ form a complete basis, so $\eta$ is traded for coefficients $C_{\Delta,J}(P)$.</span> <span data-covers="sec-conformal_basis-p2">Elements at $\Delta$ and its shadow $d-\Delta$ are related, so half the coefficients are redundant.</span> <span data-covers="sec-conformal_basis-p5 sec-conformal_basis-p7">The coefficients converge only for $\Delta_0>d/4$. Below it the double shadow of $\eta$ is expanded instead, and the contour is deformed to keep physical poles below $d/2$ and exclude their shadows.</span> <span data-covers="sec-conformal_basis-p11">For free fields in $2<d<4$ this affects only $J=0$, at the pole $d-2$ and its shadow 2.</span></p>""",

    "quadFree": r"""<p><span data-covers="quadFree-p1">The bi-Laplacian of the free action acts on each basis element by an eigenvalue $\lambda_{\Delta,J}$, so with orthogonality the quadratic action becomes diagonal: one term $\lambda_{\Delta,J}N_{\Delta,J}\,C_{\Delta,J}C_{d-\Delta,J}$ for each spin and principal-series dimension.</span> <span data-covers="quadFree-p2">Its Gaussian integral reproduces the free two-point function of $\eta$, a check on the change of variables.</span> <span data-covers="quadFree-p3">The cubic and higher terms cannot be diagonalized and are non-local in position and dimension, yet with any regularization their loop corrections should still cancel.</span></p>""",

    "sec-mapping_to_the_bulk": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-mapping_to_the_bulk-p1 section-4.1-p3">Transverse traceless bulk fields of spin $J$, expanded in bulk-to-boundary propagators on the principal series, carry the same labels as the CFT coefficients: a dimension, a spin and a boundary point.</span> <span data-covers="sec-cft_to_ads-p1 sec-cft_to_ads-p2">Identifying the two, $C^{bulk}_{\Delta,J}=f_{\Delta,J}C_{\Delta,J}$, gives a linear map from $\eta$ to the bulk fields, with $f$ constrained only by shadow and reality conditions.</span> <span data-covers="sec-cft_to_ads-p10 sec-ads_to_cft-p8">The map and its inverse are finite if $|f|$ grows like a power along the principal series.</span> <span data-covers="sec-single_trace_dual-p4 sec-single_trace_dual-p5">On-shell, boundary limits of the bulk fields give the single-trace operators; for the scalar in $d<4$ this holds off-shell.</span></p>""",

    "section-4.1": r"""<p><span data-covers="section-4.1-p1">The spin-$J$ bulk-to-boundary propagator solves the AdS wave equation with $M^2_{\Delta,J}=\Delta(\Delta-d)-J$, is transverse, and near the boundary falls off both as $z^{\Delta-J}$ and with the shadow power.</span> <span data-covers="section-4.1-p2">On the principal series, the propagators and their gradients are a complete basis for symmetric traceless bulk tensors, and the harmonic function $\Omega_{\Delta,J}$ builds the delta function.</span> <span data-covers="section-4.1-p3">A transverse field needs only the propagators themselves, so it is fixed by coefficients $C^{bulk}_{\Delta,J}(P)$.</span></p>""",

    "sec-cft_to_ads": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-cft_to_ads-p1 sec-cft_to_ads-p2">Conformal symmetry allows $C^{bulk}_{\Delta,J}=f_{\Delta,J}C_{\Delta,J}$ for any $f$ whose ratio to its shadow matches the CFT and bulk shadow coefficients, and that is real up to a factor $i^J$.</span> <span data-covers="sec-cft_to_ads-p4 sec-cft_to_ads-p5">For $d>4$ the map is a direct integral of $\eta$; for $d<4$ it acts on the bi-Laplacian of $\eta$.</span> <span data-covers="sec-cft_to_ads-p6 sec-cft_to_ads-p10">Defined with a cutoff on the principal series that is removed last, the map is a distribution, finite whenever $f$ grows like a power times a phase.</span></p>""",

    "sec-bulk_bc": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-bulk_bc-p2">A bulk field built from principal-series modes falls off at least as $z^{d/2-J}$ near the boundary, off-shell; on-shell, poles can make it faster.</span> <span data-covers="sec-bulk_bc-p3">For the scalar in $d<4$, the deformed contour adds a slower $z^{d-2}$ mode, and the bulk expansion is modified to include it.</span> <span data-covers="sec-bulk_bc-p4">If $\eta$ decays when both points go far away, the bulk fields decay at large $|x|$; configurations that do not decay need another map.</span></p>""",

    "sec-ads_to_cft": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-ads_to_cft-p1 sec-ads_to_cft-p2">Because the map is linear it can be inverted, by projecting bulk fields onto bulk-to-boundary modes; the projection converges except for the scalar in $d<4$, whose $z^{d-2}$ mode makes it diverge.</span> <span data-covers="sec-ads_to_cft-p3 sec-ads_to_cft-p4">For $d>4$ the inverse is an explicit integral; for the $d<4$ scalar it first acts with $(\nabla^2-M^2_{d-2,0})(\nabla^2-M^2_{d,0})$, which removes the divergence.</span> <span data-covers="sec-ads_to_cft-p8">Like the direct map, it is finite if $1/f$ grows at most like a power.</span></p>""",

    "sec-single_trace_dual": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-single_trace_dual-p1 sec-single_trace_dual-p2">Single-trace operators $S^J$, the scalar $\phi^*\phi$ and the conserved currents, are coincident limits of derivatives of $\eta$. On-shell each equals $1/f_{d-2+J,J}$ times the boundary limit of $z^{2-d}\Phi_J$.</span> <span data-covers="sec-single_trace_dual-p4">Off-shell the two limits need delicate cancellations, but inside correlators the contours close on the pole at $d-2+J$, which gives the relation.</span> <span data-covers="sec-single_trace_dual-p5">For the scalar in $d<4$ the pole at $d-2$ is the only contribution, so the relation holds off-shell and survives deformations of the theory.</span></p>""",

    "sec-quadratic_action": r"""<p><span data-covers="section-5.1-p1 section-5.1-p2">Substituting the map into the bi-local quadratic action gives a non-local bulk kernel, which becomes local, with four derivatives, when $f_{\Delta,J}f_{d-\Delta,J}=(-1)^J$.</span> <span data-covers="section-5.2-p3 section-5.2-p4">Its two-point function is written with transverse bulk-to-bulk propagators $\Pi^{TT}$, which stay finite for massless fields.</span> <span data-covers="section-5.3-p1 section-5.3-p2">Each spin carries a physical field of dimension $d-2+J$, massless for $J>0$, and a wrong-sign field of dimension $d+J$; for $U(N)$ these match Vasiliev's fields and the ghosts of their gauge fixing.</span> <span data-covers="section-5.3-p3">For $O(N)$ the ghost spins do not match.</span></p>""",

    "section-5.1": r"""<p><span data-covers="section-5.1-p1">For a general normalization the quadratic bulk action is a non-local kernel: harmonic functions weighted by $\lambda_{\Delta,J}/(f_{\Delta,J}f_{d-\Delta,J})$.</span> <span data-covers="section-5.1-p2 section-5.1-p3">Choosing $f_{\Delta,J}f_{d-\Delta,J}=(-1)^J$, solved by a unit-modulus $f^{\rm local}$, the completeness relation makes it local: $\Phi_J(\nabla^2-M^2_{d+J-2,J})(\nabla^2-M^2_{d+J,J})\Phi_J$, summed over spins.</span> <span data-covers="section-5.1-p4">Other local choices keep the first pole, the physical one, but may move the second.</span></p>""",

    "section-5.2": r"""<p><span data-covers="section-5.2-p1 section-5.2-p2">The standard massive spin-$J$ bulk-to-bulk propagator is transverse only up to contact terms, and it diverges as the field becomes massless, as gauge fields do.</span> <span data-covers="section-5.2-p3">Keeping only its gauge-invariant, transverse part defines $\Pi^{TT}$, which stays finite in the massless limit.</span> <span data-covers="section-5.2-p4">This propagator obeys the wave equation with a transverse delta function and, for small enough $\Delta$, has the standard boundary fall-off, so it suits the paper's transverse fields.</span></p>""",

    "section-5.3": r"""<p><span data-covers="section-5.3-p1">Inverting the quadratic action with the local $f$ gives a propagator $\propto\Pi^{TT}_{d-2+J,J}-\Pi^{TT}_{d+J,J}$: a physical spin-$J$ particle, massless for $J>0$, and a wrong-sign one that is subleading at the boundary.</span> <span data-covers="section-5.3-p2">For $U(N)$ the physical particles are exactly Vasiliev's, the graviton included, and the wrong-sign ones have the quantum numbers of the Faddeev–Popov ghosts from gauge-fixing them.</span> <span data-covers="section-5.3-p3">For $O(N)$ Vasiliev's ghosts would have odd spin and these have even spin, an unexplained mismatch.</span> <span data-covers="section-5.3-p4">The paper claims its action as the off-shell definition of Vasiliev's theory in this gauge, perturbatively in $1/N$.</span></p>""",

    "sec-bulk_theory": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-bulk_theory-p1 section-6.2-p3">The higher bi-local terms map to bulk vertices that are non-local for any $f$, keeping their powers of $N$ and their counter-terms proportional to $V$.</span> <span data-covers="section-6.1-p2 section-6.1-p3">Writing both maps through bulk bi-local functions gives two diagram identities: one removes internal double lines, the other closes a loop into a factor $V$.</span> <span data-covers="section-6.3-p3 section-6.3-p4">With them the one-loop bulk tadpole cancels its counter-term exactly, and any diagram reduces to a tree times powers of $V$, so the bi-local cancellations carry over.</span> <span data-covers="section-6.3-p5">Radial and IR cutoffs give $V\propto L^d/\varepsilon^d$.</span></p>""",

    "section-6.1": r"""<p><span data-covers="section-6.1-p2">Writing CFT three-point functions as bulk Witten diagrams turns both maps into integrals over bulk points, built from bulk bi-local functions $\theta_J$, $\tilde\theta_J$ and $\Omega_0$.</span> <span data-covers="section-6.1-p2">Their product $\Theta_J$ does not depend on the normalization $f$, and composing the two maps gives the transverse delta function.</span> <span data-covers="section-6.1-p3">In diagram notation two identities follow, proved in an appendix: one eliminates $\Theta_J$ between four $\Omega_0$ lines, the other turns a closed $\Theta_J$ loop into the factor $V$.</span></p>""",

    "section-6.2": r"""<p><span data-covers="section-6.2-p2">Each bi-local vertex ${\rm Tr}((G_0^{-1}\eta)^n)$ becomes a ring of $n$ lines $\Omega_0$, with $\theta_J$ legs ending on the bulk fields.</span> <span data-covers="section-6.2-p2">Inverting the quadratic vertex with the identities gives the bulk propagator, the two-point function of the previous section in this language.</span> <span data-covers="section-6.2-p3">The cubic and higher vertices keep their factors $N^{1-n/2}$ and the counter-terms their factor $V$; for $O(N)$ the vertices are halved, the propagator doubled and the spins even.</span></p>""",

    "section-6.3": r"""<p><span data-covers="section-6.3-p2">Tree diagrams depend on $f$ only through their external legs, as the linearity of the map requires.</span> <span data-covers="section-6.3-p3">The one-loop tadpole reduces, by the identities, to $V$ times a tree diagram, and cancels the linear counter-term exactly, as in the bi-local theory.</span> <span data-covers="section-6.3-p4">Every loop can be disentangled the same way into a power of $V$ times a tree, so the bi-local combinatorics should carry over.</span> <span data-covers="section-6.3-p5">With radial and IR cutoffs all diagrams are claimed finite, with divergences only in $V\propto L^d/\varepsilon^d$.</span></p>""",

    "sec-massDef": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-massDef-p1">A mass $m$ for the scalars gives $\eta$ an expectation value that does not decay at infinity, so the map of the earlier sections does not apply to it.</span> <span data-covers="sec-smallmass-p1 sec-smallmass-p2">A small mass, $m^2\sim1/\sqrt N$, acts only at the boundary of AdS and turns on the bulk scalar, $\langle\Phi_0\rangle\propto m^2z^2$.</span> <span data-covers="sec-NDbasisCFT-p3 sec-NDbasisBulk-p2">For finite mass, configurations that do not decay are mapped separately, by Mellin transforms in $|x_1-x_2|$ and in $z$.</span> <span data-covers="sec-finmass-p2">In $d=3$ the resulting bulk expectation value is computed numerically, as a function of $mz$ alone.</span></p>""",

    "sec-smallmass": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-smallmass-p1">A small, position-dependent mass couples to $\eta(x,x)$, which maps to the boundary limit of the bulk scalar, so the deformation acts only at the boundary of AdS.</span> <span data-covers="sec-smallmass-p1">At tree level the bulk expectation value is the mass profile smeared with the bulk-to-boundary propagator of dimension $d-2$, and only the scalar responds.</span> <span data-covers="sec-smallmass-p2">For constant mass it is $\propto m^2z^2$, a function of $mz$ alone, set by the shadow dimension 2 of the mass operator.</span></p>""",

    "sec-NDbasisCFT": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-NDbasisCFT-p1 sec-NDbasisCFT-p2">The bi-local field is split into a part averaged over translations, depending only on $|x_1-x_2|$, and a decaying remainder that keeps the conformal basis.</span> <span data-covers="sec-NDbasisCFT-p3 sec-NDbasisCFT-p4">The averaged part expands in powers of $|x_1-x_2|$ by a Mellin transform, the position-independent limit of the conformal basis, with no shadow redundancy and convergent in every $d$.</span> <span data-covers="sec-NDbasisCFT-p5">Its quadratic action is proportional to the spacetime volume, so these modes are not dynamical.</span></p>""",

    "sec-NDbasisBulk": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-NDbasisBulk-p1">In the bulk, the part of the scalar that depends only on $z$ expands by the same Mellin transform, in powers $z^{d-\Delta}$.</span> <span data-covers="sec-NDbasisBulk-p2">Matching coefficients with the factor $f_{d-\Delta,0}$ gives the non-decaying map and its inverse; without that factor the map would simply set $|x_1-x_2|=z$.</span> <span data-covers="sec-NDbasisBulk-p4 sec-NDbasisBulk-p5">With the local $f$ its quadratic action is local and its propagator an explicit power law; since the two parts recombine linearly, the bulk action and its loops are unchanged.</span></p>""",

    "sec-finmass": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-finmass-p1">For $d=3$ the massive bi-local expectation value, $\sqrt N(e^{-m|x_{12}|}-1)/(4\pi|x_{12}|)$, has a Mellin transform that diverges at short distance on the original contour.</span> <span data-covers="sec-finmass-p2">Shifting the contour and accounting for the pole it no longer encloses gives the bulk expectation value as a contour integral plus a pole term, a function of $mz$ only.</span> <span data-covers="sec-finmass-p2">The integral, complicated by the branch cuts of the local $f$, is computed numerically.</span></p>""",

    "sec-bulk_deform": r"""<p><span data-covers="critUN-p1 critUN-p2">The quartic interaction $(\sum_I|\phi_I|^2)^2$ is a double-trace term $\lambda\eta(x,x)^2$; with a Hubbard–Stratonovich field $\sigma$, bubble sums give its leading large-$N$ propagators.</span> <span data-covers="critUN-p3">For $2<d<4$ the infrared limit $\lambda\to\infty$ is the critical model, in which $\phi^*\phi$ is replaced by its shadow $\sigma$, of dimension 2.</span> <span data-covers="critUNbulk-p2 critUNbulk-p3">In the bulk only the scalar propagator changes, from $\Pi^{TT}_{d-2,0}$ to $\Pi^{TT}_{2,0}$, a different boundary condition; since this change is free of short-distance singularities, the same action gives the critical model at all orders.</span></p>""",

    "critUN": r"""<p><span data-covers="critUN-p1">In bi-local variables the quartic interaction is $\lambda\eta(x,x)^2$, plus a constant and a mass term removed by a counter-term.</span> <span data-covers="critUN-p2">A Hubbard–Stratonovich field $\sigma$ makes it linear, and a geometric series of bubble diagrams gives the leading propagators of $\sigma$ and of $\eta$.</span> <span data-covers="critUN-p3">For $2<d<4$ and $\lambda\to\infty$, $\sigma$ forces $\eta(x,x)$ to vanish and has dimension 2, so at leading order the critical theory is the free one with $\phi^*\phi$, of dimension $d-2$, replaced by its shadow.</span></p>""",

    "critUNbulk": r"""<p><span data-covers="critUNbulk-p1">The off-shell boundary relation turns the double-trace term into a boundary term, which changes only the scalar's bulk propagator.</span> <span data-covers="critUNbulk-p2">At $\lambda\to\infty$ its physical part $\Pi^{TT}_{d-2,0}$ is replaced by the shadow $\Pi^{TT}_{2,0}$, which solves the same equation with the other boundary condition.</span> <span data-covers="critUNbulk-p3">The difference is free of short-distance singularities, so, by an argument of Giombi and Yin, the free bulk rules with this propagator give the critical model at all orders in $1/N$.</span> <span data-covers="critUNbulk-p4">The $z^{d-2}$ mode is then absent, and $\sigma$ is read off from the $z^2$ term of $\Phi_0$.</span></p>""",

    "app-emb_form": r"""<p><span data-covers="app-emb_form-p1">AdS$_{d+1}$ is the hyperboloid $X^2=-1$ and flat space the null cone $P^2=0$ in $\mathbb R^{d+1,1}$, where conformal transformations and AdS isometries act linearly.</span> <span data-covers="section-A.1-p2 section-A.2-p2">Symmetric traceless tensors are encoded as polynomials in auxiliary null vectors, $Z$ on the boundary and $W$ in the bulk, with operators $D_Z$ and $K_W$ that recover their components.</span></p>""",

    "sec-free_4_point": r"""<p><span data-covers="sec-free_4_point-p2">Gaussian integration over the $C_{\Delta,J}$ gives the $\eta$ two-point function as an integral over conformal partial waves.</span> <span data-covers="sec-free_4_point-p2">Closing the contour picks the poles at $\Delta=d-2+J$, and the sum over spins of the resulting blocks reproduces the free Wick contraction, with its symmetrized partner for $O(N)$.</span></p>""",

    "bulkIdentities": r"""<p><span data-covers="section-C.1-p2">The identity that eliminates $\Theta_J$ lines is the conformal completeness relation, rewritten with Witten diagrams.</span> <span data-covers="app-bulk_dive-p1">Gluing the same relation into a loop gives a divergent factor, regularized by bulk cutoffs into $V\sim L^d/\varepsilon^d$, with the spin sum done before the cutoffs are removed.</span> <span data-covers="app-d4_mod-p7 app-d4_mod-p8">For $d<4$ the maps become differential operators, but the identities hold up to factors that cancel, and the results continue from $d>4$.</span></p>""",

    "section-C.1": r"""<p><span data-covers="section-C.1-p2">Writing each three-point function in the completeness relation as a Witten diagram turns it into a statement about $\Theta_J$ between bulk-to-boundary legs.</span> <span data-covers="section-C.1-p2">Attaching bulk-to-boundary propagators to the four boundary points and integrating over them gives the four-$\Omega$ identity.</span></p>""",

    "app-bulk_dive": r"""<p><span data-covers="app-bulk_dive-p1">Closing two legs of the completeness relation into a loop produces $\int dP\,\delta(0)$, the same divergence as the lattice count $V$ of the bi-local theory.</span> <span data-covers="app-bulk_dive-p1">In the bulk a radial cutoff and an IR cutoff regularize it to $V\sim L^d/\varepsilon^d$; the sum over spins must come first, since the dominant spins grow as the cutoffs are removed.</span></p>""",

    "app-d4_mod": r"""<p><span data-covers="app-d4_mod-p1 app-d4_mod-p2">For $d<4$ the scalar part of the inverse map, and the direct map, become differential operators.</span> <span data-covers="app-d4_mod-p4 app-d4_mod-p6">The invertibility, four-$\Omega$ and loop identities still hold, up to factors $a_{\Delta_0}^2$ that cancel in the Feynman rules.</span> <span data-covers="app-d4_mod-p8 app-d4_mod-p9">Integrating the modified vertices by parts leaves divergent boundary terms, which act as counter-terms, so results agree with those continued from $d>4$; in the critical theory no such terms arise.</span></p>""",
}
